INT. SUGAR MILL - BOILING FLOOR - NIGHT

A cavernous hall of copper vats, rafters lost in steam. A table has been dragged to the center, a thick plank on two barrels. Folding chairs ring it. Crates of burlap sacks serve as bleachers. Lanterns hang from hooks.

JETHRO MALLORY, 40s, shirtless under a leather apron, shoulders like sacks of grain, sits at one side of the table. Across from him, PILAR ESCOBEDO, 20s, rolled sleeves, braid down her back, calm as a tide pool.

Between them, HENRIK SOLBERG, 60s, referee, rubs his hands in chalk and sets them on top of theirs.

HENRIK
Elbows on the pads. Grips locked.

Jethro grips. Pilar grips. Their knuckles whiten.

HENRIK (CONT'D)
Ready?

JETHRO
Born ready.

PILAR
Skip the speech.

HENRIK
Go!

Both tense. The table groans. A vein rises on Jethro's neck. Pilar's jaw clenches. Neither moves.

The crowd begins a low chant: Jeth-ro. Jeth-ro.

PILAR
(through teeth)
You sweat a lot.

JETHRO
It's a sweaty room.

PILAR
Your wife make that apron?

JETHRO
My mother.

PILAR
She loves you.

JETHRO
Shut up.

Henrik crouches, eyes on the hands. Sweat drips from Jethro's nose onto the plank.

Pilar shifts her weight. Her free hand grips the table's edge. Jethro's arm trembles, a small tremor. Hers does too.

They breathe together, in, out.

HENRIK
Hold your lines.

Jethro surges. His hand pushes hers an inch toward the pad. The crowd roars.

PILAR
(gasping)
Not yet.

She drives back. His arm straightens. Equal again.

Both freeze. Both stare. Neither blinks.

JETHRO
You're good.

PILAR
My father taught me. At a mill like this.

JETHRO
Where?

PILAR
Matanzas.

JETHRO
I had a cousin in Matanzas.

PILAR
Everyone does.

They laugh, tight and brief. The effort cracks it.

Jethro lunges again. The table skids across the floor. Henrik leaps clear. Chairs topple. The crowd scatters back as the barrels shift.

Pilar twists, wrenches her shoulder, slams the back of his hand toward the pad. It stops an inch from the wood. Both grunt.

HENRIK
Hold! Hold! The table!

They don't hold. They are locked. The table tilts. They tumble together onto the sacks, hands still clasped, wrestling.

Henrik blows a whistle. No one hears.

PILAR
(rolling)
Pad's behind you!

JETHRO
Is not!

HENRIK
It is! It's behind you both!

They tumble over the barrels, bounce against a vat, freeze. Steam hisses. Their joined hands hover above a sack of raw sugar. Neither willing to break.

PILAR
Truce?

JETHRO
Till dawn.

PILAR
Dawn's fine.

They let go together. Both slump on the sacks, panting.

HENRIK
(to the crowd)
Draw! The match is a draw!

A cheer, ragged. Jethro and Pilar lie there staring at the rafters, arms spread, hands shaking.

JETHRO
Again?

PILAR
Tomorrow.

JETHRO
Bring your father.

PILAR
He's dead.

JETHRO
Then bring his arm.

Pilar smiles, for the first time. She sits up and reaches for the table.

HENRIK
You people are insane.

She slams her elbow on the plank. Jethro hauls himself up and does the same. The crowd goes silent.

HENRIK (CONT'D)
(sighing)
Ready?
